# TrueCost Elite Mobile UI Research and Redesign Blueprint

## What the strongest mobile design research actually says

There is no defensible single “world #1 mobile app UI” ranking. The strongest current benchmark is a combination of **platform design awards, first-party design systems, accessibility guidance, and successful consumer-product patterns**. In June 2026, Apple’s Design Awards selected 12 winners from 36 finalists specifically across categories including Interaction, Delight and Fun, Innovation, Inclusivity, and Visuals and Graphics. For TrueCost, the most relevant 2026 references are **Moonlitt**, winner for Interaction, and **Tide Guide**, winner for Visuals and Graphics. Apple specifically praised Moonlitt for an elegant interface, easy onboarding, and strong Liquid Glass integration; Tide Guide was recognized for crisp data presentation, custom animations, cohesive theming, and Liquid Glass used in a practical rather than decorative way. citeturn2view0

Google’s current direction points the same way. Material 3 Expressive, current in 2026, explicitly combines **motion, shape, typography, color, and adaptive components** to create interfaces that feel more emotional, distinctive, and desirable rather than merely functional. Google describes it as research-backed and says the system expands Material 3 with expressive motion, flexible typography, contrasting shapes, and richer theming. citeturn8search2turn8search3 Google Play’s 2025 awards also rewarded apps such as Focus Friend for a simple but emotionally engaging experience, while the India winner District was highlighted as an urban utility built around personalized discovery rather than overwhelming users with raw data. citeturn4search0turn4search1

The key finding for TrueCost is therefore:

> **The best modern UI is not “maximum glass + maximum animation + maximum images.” It is maximum craftsmanship, with carefully selected moments of visual richness.**

Apple’s current design principles explicitly recommend focusing the interface around its primary purpose, staying out of the user’s way, maintaining consistency, providing clear feedback, and remaining transparent about what the product is doing. citeturn3search11 Apple’s iPhone guidance similarly recommends limiting on-screen controls so users can concentrate on primary content and tasks while keeping secondary actions discoverable. citeturn3search4

This is especially important for TrueCost because your product spans a very wide information space: Grocery, Food, Pharmacy, Shopping, Mobility, Travel, Finance, Productivity/SaaS and dozens of providers. Your repository discovery confirms that TrueCost is already structurally conceived as a multi-category system with 32 target providers, connectors and mobile acquisition components, so the visual system needs to make those heterogeneous domains feel like **one premium product rather than eight apps stitched together**. fileciteturn0file0

Research on aesthetics is also useful here. Studies have found relationships between interface aesthetics, appeal, perceived usability and even task performance, although the literature does **not** support the simplistic idea that making something beautiful automatically fixes usability. Some experiments find positive effects from aesthetics, while others show usability itself can strongly influence perceived beauty. citeturn5search0turn5search4turn5search7 A 2023 smartphone-interface study found a more attractive interface improved subjective experience and was associated with better task performance in that experiment. citeturn5search11

For TrueCost, that means the target is not:

```text
FUNCTIONAL APP
+
GLASSMORPHISM
+
GRADIENT
+
LOTS OF ANIMATIONS
=
PREMIUM
```

It should be:

```text
