## Glass, imagery, logos and motion should be used very differently

### Glassmorphism: yes, but not everywhere

This is probably the most important finding from the research.

Apple’s current guidance for Liquid Glass says it is intended to create a **distinct functional layer for controls and navigation above content**. Apple explicitly says not to use Liquid Glass throughout the content layer and recommends applying it sparingly because overuse produces unnecessary complexity and distracting hierarchy. citeturn0search4

So for TrueCost:

**Excellent uses of glass**

```text
Bottom navigation
Floating search controls
Top floating action bar
Comparison filter/control bar
Context menus
Transient sheets
Selected interactive control
Floating “Ask TrueCost” action
```

**Bad uses of glass**

```text
Every product card
Every provider row
Every price
Every category
Every settings group
Every result
Large blocks of text
Dense finance information
Pharmacy information
```

The content itself should mostly live on crisp white or lightly tinted surfaces.

Conceptually:

```text
CONTENT
─────────────────────────────

Product image

Red Bull Energy Drink
250 ml

₹125

Blinkit      12 min


─────────────────────────────

     translucent control layer

 ╭─────────────────────────╮
 │ Home  Search  Saved  Me │
 ╰─────────────────────────╯
```

Apple’s 2026 Design Award recognition of Moonlitt and Tide Guide is particularly instructive: both use Liquid Glass, yet Apple praised them for elegant interaction and coherent content presentation—not for simply covering the interface in transparency. citeturn2view0

For your React Native/Expo frontend, `expo-blur` is now capable of native blur on Android as well as iOS, with current Expo documentation describing typical uses such as navigation bars, tab bars, and modals. citeturn6search13 That happens to align almost perfectly with where Apple says this visual treatment belongs.

### Images should carry emotion, not clutter

Use images aggressively **where images communicate what the user is deciding about**.

That means:

| Category | Image intensity |
|---|---|
| Grocery | Medium |
| Food | Very high |
| Pharmacy | Low / controlled |
| Shopping | Very high |
| Mobility | Map/vehicle/contextual |
| Travel | Very high |
| Finance | Low |
| SaaS | Moderate |

A shopping result can have a beautifully staged product image.

A food page should look appetizing.

A travel result can have cinematic destination imagery.

A pharmacy result should **not** become an illustration-heavy wellness fantasy that hides dosage information.

Finance should feel sophisticated, not decorated.

Apple explicitly warns that text over imagery can damage both image clarity and text legibility and recommends sufficient contrast/background treatment when overlays are used. citeturn3search0

For your Expo implementation, `expo-image` is particularly appropriate because current documentation provides native disk and memory caching, BlurHash/ThumbHash placeholders, content-fit positioning, broad image-format support and source transitions that reduce flicker. citeturn6search11

So a premium image sequence should be:

```text
placeholder
   ↓
BlurHash / ThumbHash
   ↓
image fade-in
   ↓
high-resolution cached image
```

rather than:

```text
empty grey box
   ↓
sudden image pop
```

### Provider logos must be authentic, but legally clean

I completely agree with your product instinct that seeing real Blinkit, Zepto, Amazon, Flipkart, Uber, Zomato, MakeMyTrip, Slack, Figma and similar brands will dramatically improve recognizability.

However:

> **“Real logo” must mean official, approved asset—not an SVG copied from Google Images.**

Brand usage terms can be strict. Slack, for example, provides official downloadable assets but explicitly prohibits distorting, cropping, recoloring, applying effects to, or animating its logo. citeturn7search2turn7search3 Figma likewise publishes official trademark/logo resources. citeturn7search5 Amazon’s published brand guidance shows that third-party uses can have contextual restrictions and, in some situations, approval requirements. citeturn7search16

Therefore build:

```text
ProviderBrandRegistry
```

containing:

```text
providerId
displayName
officialLogoAsset
assetSource
brandGuidelineSource
lightSurfaceVariant
darkSurfaceVariant
minimumClearSpace
allowedBackgrounds
trademarkRestrictions
fallbackInitials
```

And animate this:

```text
╭────────────────────╮
│ [Slack logo] Slack │
╰────────────────────╯
      ↑
container scales/fades
```

rather than modifying the actual provider logo artwork.

This is especially important because TrueCost eventually presents many providers side-by-side; each logo needs equal **visual weight**, not identical physical dimensions.

### Animation should become part of the brand

The biggest UI upgrade opportunity is not adding animation everywhere.

It is creating a **TrueCost motion language**.

Material 3 Expressive explicitly emphasizes motion physics, shape transformation, flexible typography and motion as mechanisms for creating more engaging products. citeturn8search2turn8search10 Apple similarly recognizes top-tier animation as part of award-winning visual design, as seen in Tide Guide’s animated charts. citeturn2view0

I would define four motion classes:

```text
MICRO
100–180 ms
press / selection / opacity / icon

QUICK
180–260 ms
chip / provider selection / small card state

STANDARD
260–360 ms
sheet / card / search transformation / result reveal

EXPRESSIVE
380–520 ms
winner reveal / hero transition / important screen transition
```

Those timings are proposed TrueCost design tokens, not universal laws.

Use springs for tactile interaction:

```text
tap
↓
0.97 scale
↓
release spring
↓
1.00
```

Use subtle staggered result entry:

```text
Recommendation
       ↓ 40ms
Provider 1
       ↓ 40ms
Provider 2
       ↓ 40ms
Provider 3
```

Use number motion intelligently:

```text
₹899
 ↓
₹842
```

when filters or benefits change.

Use shape transformation:

```text
search capsule
      ↓
active search
      ↓
results control bar
```

Use motion to preserve spatial continuity:

```text
product card image
       ↓
product detail hero

provider row
       ↓
provider breakdown sheet
```

For your current React Native environment, Reanimated supports scroll-driven and layout animation primitives, while its performance documentation specifically warns against animating excessive numbers of components and against inefficient synchronization with the JavaScript thread. citeturn6search5turn6search8 Some newer shared-element functionality remains experimental/not yet production-ready in current Reanimated documentation, so I would not redesign your entire navigation system around experimental shared-element transitions. citeturn6search2turn6search4

Use stable transforms, opacity, layout transitions, scroll interpolation and sheet animations first.

Haptics should accompany only meaningful interactions such as selection changes, winner confirmation and significant success/error events; Expo currently provides cross-platform haptic APIs for Android and iOS. citeturn6search16turn6search17

And importantly, motion must adapt to accessibility preferences. Apple explicitly advises reducing automatic/repetitive movement, replacing large spatial transitions with fades where appropriate, and avoiding animated depth/blur when Reduce Motion is enabled. citeturn3search2turn3search9

So TrueCost needs:

```text
MotionMode.FULL
MotionMode.REDUCED
```

not just:

```text
animation = always
```

