### Use performance-oriented primitives

For production imagery, Expo’s image component already provides caching and placeholders designed to improve image delivery. citeturn6search11

For gradients:

```text
expo-linear-gradient
```

provides a native cross-platform gradient surface. citeturn6search1

For glass:

```text
expo-blur
```

but only on a handful of layers. citeturn6search13

For motion:

```text
Reanimated
```

using UI-thread/shared-value patterns and avoiding mass simultaneous animation, following its performance guidance. citeturn6search8

For haptics:

```text
expo-haptics
```

only at significant moments. citeturn6search17

### Accessibility is part of elite design

Android recommends interactive touch areas of at least 48×48dp, including for custom controls. citeturn1search0 Apple similarly emphasizes appropriately sized controls, sufficient contrast, larger text support, VoiceOver and interfaces that do not rely on color alone. citeturn3search1

So the visual QA gate should include:

```text
minimum touch target ≥ 48dp
Dynamic Type / font scaling
TalkBack
VoiceOver
Reduce Motion
Increase Contrast
no color-only state communication
no glass destroying readability
```

An app that looks extraordinary at one fixed font size but collapses with accessibility settings is not elite design.

